            "text": "{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"scope\",\n      \"location\": \"(x : Fin n → ℝ)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The formal proposition fixes x before choosing N, c, and a, so those witnesses may depend on x. “Expressed identically” suggests choosing one representation that works for every x.\",\n      \"prediction\": \"The original proposition may be provable without producing uniform witnesses. An equivalence proof would show that this quantifier difference does not change the mathematical result, but would not remove the translation question.\",\n      \"probe_kind\": \"equivalence\",\n      \"comparison\": \"∀ (n : ℕ), n > 0 → ∀ (avals : ℕ → (ℕ → Fin n → ℝ) → Prop), (∀ (N : ℕ) (a : ℕ → Fin n → ℝ), avals N a ↔ ∀ (i : Fin N) (j : Fin n), (a i j = -1 ∨ a i j = 0 ∨ a i j = 1)) → ∃ (N : ℕ) (c : Fin N → ℚ) (a : ℕ → Fin n → ℝ), avals N a ∧ ∀ (x : Fin n → ℝ), (∏ i : Fin n, x i) = ∑ i : Fin N, c i * (∑ j : Fin n, a i j * x j) ^ n\",\n      \"bridge\": \"translated\",\n      \"obligation\": \"Review whether “expressed identically” requires N, c, and a to be independent of x; mathematical equivalence alone would not settle that translation choice.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The testable suspicion is the scope of x relative to the existential witnesses.\"\n}"
